WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.920
- I bet the judge is gonna
say, "I'll allow it,"

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:03.060
at some point in this hearing.

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:03.893
I guarantee it.
- Friendly even,

00:00:03.893 --> 00:00:05.910
but after the last custody hearing,

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:07.183
Michael was pretty worried about--

00:00:07.183 --> 00:00:09.990
- Objection, Your Honor.
- On what grounds?

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:10.940
- Hearsay.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:12.470
- Nice try, Mrs. Florrick.

00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:13.303
I'll allow it.

00:00:13.303 --> 00:00:14.360
Go ahead, Mrs. Lewis.
- Ah, called it!

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.943
(bright music)

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:21.320
Hey, Legal Eagles, D. James Stone here,

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:24.170
teaching you how to think like a lawyer.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:25.450
Today, we're gonna tackle one

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:27.530
of the most requested TV shows out there,

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.180
and that's The Good Wife.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.580
As one subscriber put it,

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:33.630
it is the most highly rated legal TV show

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:34.650
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.520
So that should stand for something.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:37.980
And fun fact,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.030
this is the most highly
requested legal TV show

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.600
from my mom.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.340
So Mom, I'm finally getting around

00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:47.980
to watching The Good Wife.

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:49.450
I'm doing it for you.

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:51.320
And be sure to stick around until the end,

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.620
where I give this episode
of The Good Wife a grade

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.080
for legal realism and how accurate it is

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.960
to real life lawyering.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.700
So without any further
ado, let's get started

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.929
and dig right into The Good Wife.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:03.770
(associates laughing)

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:05.150
- So we'll need some of you to help out

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:06.660
with the lower-profile client work

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.100
to free up our top litigators.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.870
- Ed, you take the witness
prep on highway redistribution.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.880
Don, you take the Brighton criminal.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.658
And Alicia will take the pro bono.

00:01:14.658 --> 00:01:16.430
(Alicia sighs)
Everyone else, your task is

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:19.610
to show Sheffrin-Marks our A game, okay?

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.780
- Okay, so the main
character is being assigned

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:22.860
with pro bono work.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.270
It's not uncommon for large
law firms to get a huge case.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.150
It's all hands on deck,
so they really pull

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.200
in all the bodies they can.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.720
And as the lawyer mentioned,

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.050
it does tend to help
with year-end bonuses.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:36.440
If you work a lot of billable hours,

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.890
then you'll get a lot of year-end bonuses.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:43.460
But the main character is
getting assigned pro bono.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:47.680
Now pro bono, as you may
know, is work that's provided

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.240
by a law firm for free.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:51.360
Usually, it's for indigent clients.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.820
But generally, law firms,
and sometimes it's mandated

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.010
by the State, have to do a certain number

00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:58.740
of pro bono hours per year.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.520
It's a way for the law firm to give back,

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.240
and it's a way to help those

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.730
who can't afford really expensive lawyers

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.250
and legal services.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:09.720
The lawyers will provide that for free,

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.720
and that's called pro bono.

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:13.310
So it looks like everybody
else is being thrown

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:14.660
on this huge class action,

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.920
whereas the main character is being given

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.300
the leftover pro bono work,
which is not uncommon.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.133
- [Receptionist] Lockhart and Gardner.

00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:23.060
- So Will speaks highly of you.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.970
He says you graduated top
of your class at Georgetown.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:27.310
When was this?

00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:28.660
- 15 years ago.
- Uh-huh.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.390
And you spent two years at--

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.010
- Crozier, Abrams and Abbott.
- Good firm.

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:35.380
Will says you clocked the
highest billable hours there.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:36.590
Why'd you leave?

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.270
- Okay, that's a funny throwaway line.

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:39.103
They're trying to imply

00:02:39.103 --> 00:02:41.780
that the main character is
a really diligent worker.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:45.150
The way it works at
really big law firms is

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.040
that the lawyers are all on salaries.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:48.580
So at the end of the year,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:50.280
they'll all take home the same amount,

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.080
except if there's a big bonus.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.280
And the way that you get a big bonus is

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:57.850
by clocking a large number of hours.

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:01.780
Not every hour that you work
at a law firm brings in money

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.710
to the law firm or to you
personally as a lawyer.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:05.820
And it's only when you're

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:09.120
in a much smaller firm does every hour

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.280
that you bill go directly
into your pocket.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.830
In large law firms, all the
attorneys billable hours go

00:03:15.830 --> 00:03:19.250
into a general pot that's
divided based on the salary.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:21.880
So they're saying that
she, back in the day,

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.080
when she was in private
practice was billing more

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:29.170
than anybody else, and in real
life, certainly someone has

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.480
to bill more hours than everybody else,

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:35.060
but those numbers can be very, very high,

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:40.060
somewhere between 2,500 and
3,000 billable hours per year.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.280
I would say for every three
to four billable hours you're

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.360
in the office, you're probably not billing

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:47.620
at least one of those hours.

00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:50.250
Whether you're eating lunch,
or you're eating dinner,

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:51.450
if you're really staying late,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:55.360
or you're just taking
a break, there are lots

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.380
of times you're when in the
office, and you're not billing.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.260
So to bill the highest
number of billable hours

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.900
in a firm is both a badge of honor

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.730
and sort of a dubious mark

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:07.750
that she's just a really,
really hard worker

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:09.810
and good for the firm
but not necessarily good

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:10.643
for her personal life.

00:04:10.643 --> 00:04:12.780
So that's an interesting little tidbit

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:14.200
that they threw in there.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.700
- Jennifer Lewis, 26 years old,

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.510
taught second-grade, accused
of killing her ex-husband.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:22.290
The prosecution thought it
was a slam dunk, 45 years,

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:24.770
but the jury came back
last week deadlocked.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.240
Six jurors voted to convict, six not.

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:29.710
I'm not even sure why the
state attorney is retrying,

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:31.082
except he wants justice!

00:04:31.082 --> 00:04:32.220
(dog whimpers)

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.330
He wants to prove himself.

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:36.180
So stick with my strategy
from the first trial.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.340
The police focused on Jennifer so early

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.190
in the investigation,
they never even looked

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:41.113
for the carjacker.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:44.780
- What she's talking about there is that

00:04:44.780 --> 00:04:47.970
when in jury trial, you
generally have 12 jurors.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.350
That's why the famous movie
is called 12 Angry Men,

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.880
because back in the
day, they were all men.

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:55.340
Now that's obviously not the
case, which is a good thing.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:58.710
But to convict someone, you
have to get all 12 jurors

00:04:58.710 --> 00:04:59.960
to vote to convict.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:03.090
If any one of those people refuses to vote

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.060
to convict the individual,
it's called a hung jury.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:07.970
Now that's different than if all 12

00:05:07.970 --> 00:05:11.860
of the jurors say not
to convict or to acquit.

00:05:11.860 --> 00:05:13.730
Then the person just goes
free, and there's no way

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.040
to retry them, generally speaking.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:19.480
Here, obviously, at least
one of the jurors found

00:05:19.480 --> 00:05:21.060
that they couldn't find her guilty.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.040
The jury hung, and in that situation,

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:26.630
the prosecutor has the ability to retry

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:28.140
that individual, at a later time.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:30.310
They go through the entire
trial all over again.

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:32.390
So that's apparently what's going on.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:34.890
This woman is going to be
tried for a second time.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:37.633
- Where's Diane?

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.470
- Diane asked me to step in for her.

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.770
Jennifer, I'm Alicia Florrick.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:45.460
I'm one of the other
lawyers with the firm.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:46.293
- Step in?

00:05:46.293 --> 00:05:47.126
For how long?

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:49.085
- For the retrial.

00:05:49.085 --> 00:05:49.918
(wood thuds)

00:05:49.918 --> 00:05:51.340
- Oh my god.
- All rise!

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:52.740
Ma'am.

00:05:52.740 --> 00:05:54.810
The Criminal Court of Cook
County is now in session.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:57.039
The Honorable Judge
Richard Cuesta presiding.

00:05:57.039 --> 00:05:58.103
(gavel thuds)
- Be seated.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:00.423
Okay, let's hear it.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:04.940
- Your Honor, I just--
- Your Honor, just

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:06.060
to refresh your memory,

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:07.740
the accused was deemed a flight risk,

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:08.970
due to an earlier custody hearing

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.090
when she threatened to
run off with her daughter.

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:12.990
- So that was a good tactic
by the prosecutor here,

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:15.260
to take the lead and to educate the judge.

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:17.120
Usually what happens when
you make an appearance

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.060
in court is there's a huge docket,

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:23.510
which means that there are
maybe a dozen or so cases

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:25.410
that have hearings to discuss

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:27.280
that morning or that afternoon.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:29.280
So number one, the judge has a lot

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:30.420
of different cases to deal with.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.920
Number two, it may have
been weeks or months

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:35.050
since you were last in the courthouse,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.240
especially if you're in state court

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:38.500
where there are just lots

00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:40.970
and lots of cases, continually churning.

00:06:40.970 --> 00:06:44.140
So now the judge will probably take a look

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:46.160
at the paperwork before taking the stand,

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.230
but again, there may
be a dozen or so cases

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:51.210
that he or she needs to
talk about, that morning.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:54.010
So it's always a good idea
to take the initiative

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.830
and to err on the side of
providing more information

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:59.750
to the judge than you
would necessarily give

00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:01.670
to someone who's very
familiar with the case,

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:04.060
'cause the judge might not remember that.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:05.530
But I think it's also to show

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.890
that the prosecutor's just
kind of taking command

00:07:07.890 --> 00:07:09.820
and being a jerk, but we'll see.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.930
- Just last week, a jury deadlocked

00:07:11.930 --> 00:07:14.500
on these murder charges,
six to six, Mr. Brody.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.060
Now I know our new state's
attorney wants to look tough,

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:19.050
but why are you fighting this?

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:23.780
- In reality, if a jury hung
six members of the jury to six,

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:26.250
meaning six wanted to vote for guilt,

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:27.920
and six wanted to acquit,

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.520
it's very unlikely the prosecutor
would try that case again.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.540
That means you have a really weak case.

00:07:32.540 --> 00:07:34.940
Usually, a hung jury is one or two people,

00:07:34.940 --> 00:07:37.540
which means that, with
a different jury pool,

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:40.070
you have a much better
chance of conviction.

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:42.800
But if you're hanging the jury six to six,

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:45.720
your case is weak, and
you probably don't want

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:46.553
to try that one again.

00:07:46.553 --> 00:07:49.313
It's just a waste of the
court's time, at that point.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.120
- If Mrs. Florrick is so intent
on getting her client out,

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:55.410
why doesn't she agree to a speedy trial?

00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:56.710
- [Richard] Mrs. Florrick?

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:58.510
- Yes, Your Honor.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:01.300
- The wife of the esteemed Peter Florrick?

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:04.320
Your husband and I never
quite saw eye-to-eye, ma'am.

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:06.350
- Your Honor.
- Mrs. Florrick, don't talk.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:07.940
But if the prosecution
thinks that this will

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.100
in some way prejudice
me against your client,

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:11.440
he is sorely mistaken.

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.420
Nice try, Matan.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:15.230
So Ms. Lewis is granted pretrial release

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:16.890
with electronic monitoring.

00:08:16.890 --> 00:08:19.930
She is restricted to temporary
housing, attorney's offices

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:23.070
and transit in between, and
given that this is a rerun,

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.150
I'll set the trial date for the 25th.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:27.050
Now are we all happy?

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:28.645
Good.

00:08:28.645 --> 00:08:29.562
- Find out what she's doing.
- You just wait.

00:08:29.562 --> 00:08:30.395
We'll have to wait.

00:08:30.395 --> 00:08:32.190
- That's a little more terse and crotchety

00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.330
than most judges are gonna be.

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:37.160
Judges know what's going
on, for the most part.

00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:39.070
And when they see a new
attorney, they're likely

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:41.960
to be a little bit more
lenient than this judge here,

00:08:41.960 --> 00:08:45.220
although he is giving the
main character, Alicia,

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:46.163
what she wants.

00:08:47.260 --> 00:08:49.430
He's sort of telling her,
you're gonna win this one,

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:50.970
so just be quiet.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:53.990
Still, you want to command
respect from all the lawyers,

00:08:53.990 --> 00:08:56.310
and so you don't want to be coming off

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:57.850
as a crotchety old person.

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.960
And so that's a little unrealistic.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:03.290
This hearing would probably
take 10 to 15 minutes,

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:06.600
not 30 seconds, but you know, the outcome

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:09.087
and the interchange is not crazy.

00:09:09.087 --> 00:09:11.235
(dramatic music)
- A man came up to Michael.

00:09:11.235 --> 00:09:12.798
(gun clicks)
(crowbar clangs)

00:09:12.798 --> 00:09:15.327
I didn't see the gun until,

00:09:15.327 --> 00:09:17.261
(grunts) Michael!
(gun fires)

00:09:17.261 --> 00:09:18.094
Michael!

00:09:19.237 --> 00:09:21.260
And I saw Michael's face.

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.625
It was, uh...

00:09:22.625 --> 00:09:25.208
(somber music)

00:09:26.470 --> 00:09:28.930
- Do you need to take a break?

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:32.040
- Uh, no, just if there was some water.

00:09:32.040 --> 00:09:32.873
- Sure.

00:09:34.836 --> 00:09:35.669
(door clacks)

00:09:35.669 --> 00:09:37.420
Sonia, we need some water in here.

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:38.500
- It's gonna be about five minutes,

00:09:38.500 --> 00:09:40.385
but then I've got to
do this depo upstairs.

00:09:40.385 --> 00:09:41.570
(Devin laughs)

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.340
- That secretary should
definitely support an attorney

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:48.370
who is conducting an interview
of a criminal defendant.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.640
That is going to trump whatever it is

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.100
that she was working on, at the moment.

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:56.390
And also, it's not unusual for secretaries

00:09:56.390 --> 00:10:00.890
to support three, four and
sometimes five attorneys

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:02.580
in a major law firm.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:04.330
It's part of the cost-cutting measures

00:10:04.330 --> 00:10:05.780
that a lot of big firms use.

00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:07.330
It's actually not very difficult

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:10.250
for assistants to provide support

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.350
to two different attorneys,
at the same time.

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:14.500
And also, I don't know
what she was supposed

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:15.640
to do with the deposition.

00:10:15.640 --> 00:10:18.220
That's not something secretaries do.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:21.930
Anyway, all right, so
Alicia's underwater, I guess.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:23.630
- Can I ask you how many voted

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:25.240
for conviction from the start, sir?

00:10:25.240 --> 00:10:26.858
- 11.
- What?

00:10:26.858 --> 00:10:28.690
- (sighs) There was only one holdout,

00:10:28.690 --> 00:10:31.000
juror number nine (chuckles).

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.480
We argued with her for three days.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.130
- But the judge polled the jury,

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:36.460
and they deadlocked six to six.

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:39.310
- Yes, well, the judge would
only declare us deadlocked

00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:40.680
if we were evenly split.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.130
So some of us agreed to change our vote

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:45.300
to not guilty, just to get out of there.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.550
If it hadn't been for juror number nine,

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.190
we would've convicted.

00:10:49.190 --> 00:10:51.060
She didn't convince us.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:53.130
She exhausted us.

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:54.330
- Ooh, that's interesting.

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:55.850
I've never heard of that happening.

00:10:55.850 --> 00:10:58.070
But that does sound like
something that could happen.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:01.070
A judge has some discretion about when

00:11:01.070 --> 00:11:04.530
to declare a particular
case as deadlocked.

00:11:04.530 --> 00:11:08.240
So it's possible that,
under some circumstances,

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:10.210
that the jury just wanted to go home,

00:11:10.210 --> 00:11:12.800
and so they would manipulate
some of their votes

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:15.530
to make it seem like a deadlock,

00:11:15.530 --> 00:11:18.200
and everyone can get out of it.

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:19.740
That wouldn't be a good thing

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:22.580
for our judicial system,
but unfortunately,

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:24.570
that sounds like something
that's plausible,

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:26.713
because jurors are human beings, too.

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:30.890
Oh and by the way, I don't
pretend to know the entire body

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:32.990
of American jurisprudence,
off the top of my head.

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:35.530
So I may get things
wrong from time to tome.

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:39.030
If you see a legal or factual inaccuracy,

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:40.940
please leave me a comment down below.

00:11:40.940 --> 00:11:42.870
But if you do, please leave me a comment

00:11:42.870 --> 00:11:45.120
in the form of an objection.

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.060
If you leave an objection,
I will get to it,

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:50.980
and I will either sustain
or overrule your objection.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:52.920
I look forward to seeing them.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:56.160
- Just so I'm clear, the
defense expert argued

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:59.470
that the gunshot residue
on Jennifer's hands came

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:00.980
from the struggle.
- Oh so sweet.

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:02.582
- Is that why you held out?

00:12:02.582 --> 00:12:03.756
- Oh I don't know about that.

00:12:03.756 --> 00:12:04.600
(cat meows)

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:06.050
I just tend to get a feeling.

00:12:06.920 --> 00:12:08.116
Don't I, Cyrus?

00:12:08.116 --> 00:12:09.570
(solemn music)

00:12:09.570 --> 00:12:11.560
- Okay, that sounds a little crazy,

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:15.260
that a juror would base
her decision based only

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:19.230
on a feeling, but a juror
can make their decision based

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:20.470
on just about anything.

00:12:20.470 --> 00:12:23.120
And I remember when I served on a jury,

00:12:23.120 --> 00:12:25.920
when I was in law school,
in a criminal case,

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:28.700
one of the jurors refused to convict,

00:12:28.700 --> 00:12:31.610
because she felt her
spirit wouldn't allow it.

00:12:31.610 --> 00:12:34.020
And she couldn't articulate
any other reasons.

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:37.570
Now (chuckles), the other
jurors and I took a step back.

00:12:37.570 --> 00:12:40.430
We realized the woman was
doing the best that she could.

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:43.690
And we talked it through, and
eventually, we all 12 decided

00:12:43.690 --> 00:12:46.620
on conviction on most
of the counts at issue.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:50.350
But people make different
decisions for different reasons.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:53.110
And that's part of the
benefit of the jury system,

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.560
is that it's a
check-and-balance to make sure

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.470
that the state doesn't overreach.

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:59.610
But the downside is that
sometimes you can get

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:04.050
some really strange
decision-making going on

00:13:04.050 --> 00:13:05.913
behind the curtain of the jury.

00:13:06.766 --> 00:13:09.210
- You know the new associate Cary?

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:11.180
- The one in the Brioni?

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:12.400
What? I'm observant.

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:14.407
- Yes, the one in the Brioni.

00:13:14.407 --> 00:13:15.240
- Brioni is a type of suit.
- He said to me,

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:16.960
"May the best man win."

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:18.660
What exactly does he mean by that.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:20.960
- What he means is something

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:22.950
I thought we weren't making public.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:23.783
- What?

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:27.480
- Look, we only have one
associate position open.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.170
So we agreed to hire two applicants,

00:13:29.170 --> 00:13:31.580
and in six months, decide
which one to retain.

00:13:31.580 --> 00:13:33.940
- So this is a contest
between me and Cary?

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:36.690
- It was either that or a cage match.

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:39.230
- That would be highly
unusual, to hire two associates

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:41.120
and then make them fight to the death,

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:43.810
especially because associates
can be profit centers

00:13:43.810 --> 00:13:44.870
for large law firms.

00:13:44.870 --> 00:13:48.880
They work a lot of hours,
and their salary is high,

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:51.370
compared to the national
average, but it's actually low,

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:54.870
compared to the revenue that
they're bringing into the firm.

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:57.150
So generally, there's no real reason

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:59.350
to make associates fight
against each other.

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:02.510
If you make an offer to
someone, at a large law firm,

00:14:02.510 --> 00:14:04.910
generally they can stick
around for quite some time,

00:14:04.910 --> 00:14:06.330
because they're good for the firm,

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:08.980
and there's no reason to get rid of them.

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:12.790
It would also be highly unusual
to tell the two associates

00:14:12.790 --> 00:14:13.870
that they're up against each other.

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.920
You wouldn't want to create bad blood

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:17.020
between those associates

00:14:17.020 --> 00:14:18.660
and all the other associates as well.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:20.770
So that's pretty unusual, to say

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:22.150
that two associates are gonna fight

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:24.080
to the death for their position.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:26.960
- See, the computer automatically
records the surveillance,

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:28.860
marks it with date and time.

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:30.340
- Is that the night of the murder?

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:33.110
- Yeah, I mean, even if the
computer did mismark it,

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:34.520
I make an hourly tour of the lot,

00:14:34.520 --> 00:14:36.360
and I didn't see no pickup truck.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:39.853
Look, that was me, 11:03,
just before the murder.

00:14:41.521 --> 00:14:44.400
- Uh, can we get copies of these?

00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:45.912
- Sure.

00:14:45.912 --> 00:14:46.745
Lanie?

00:14:47.811 --> 00:14:48.644
Lanie!

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:51.480
- I like what these attorneys are doing,

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:54.050
that they're going to the
scene of the accident,

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:56.870
and they are personally investigating.

00:14:56.870 --> 00:15:00.170
Good attorneys conduct
written discovery very well.

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:01.810
They'll ask questions on paper.

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.800
They'll get a whole lot of documents.

00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:06.390
But a great attorney really digs in

00:15:06.390 --> 00:15:08.620
and checks all the things that they can

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:10.900
to make sure that their case
is as strong as possible

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:14.050
or that the opponent's case
is as weak as possible.

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:15.770
And going to the scene of an accident

00:15:15.770 --> 00:15:18.220
or a crime is a great thing
for an attorney to do.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:19.670
Not everyone will do that.

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:21.980
So I really like what these
attorneys are doing here,

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:22.840
and they're really getting

00:15:22.840 --> 00:15:24.770
into all the details that they can.

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:26.060
- [Matan] Please state your name.

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:27.157
- [Cindy] Cindy Lewis.

00:15:27.157 --> 00:15:28.320
- [Matan] And you were
married to the victim

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:29.700
for how long, Mrs. Lewis?

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.363
- Two years, until (sighs)...

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:34.780
- Get out of the well of the court!

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:38.110
- Thank you.
- And how would you describe

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:39.170
your husband's relationship

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:40.003
with his first wife.
- I'll bet the judge

00:15:40.003 --> 00:15:41.170
is gonna say, "I'll
allow it," at some point

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:42.003
in this hearing.

00:15:42.003 --> 00:15:42.836
I guarantee it.
- Friendly even,

00:15:42.836 --> 00:15:44.560
but after last the custody hearing,

00:15:44.560 --> 00:15:45.570
Michael was pretty worried

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:47.040
about Jennifer.
- Objection, Your Honor.

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:48.610
- On what grounds?

00:15:48.610 --> 00:15:49.540
- Hearsay.

00:15:49.540 --> 00:15:50.950
- Nice try, Mrs. Florrick.

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:51.783
I'll allow it.

00:15:51.783 --> 00:15:52.870
Go ahead, Mrs. Lewis.
- Ah, called it!

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:55.270
- He was worried Jennifer was intent

00:15:55.270 --> 00:15:56.919
on getting sole custody.

00:15:56.919 --> 00:15:58.820
- Thank you, Mrs. Lewis.

00:15:58.820 --> 00:15:59.990
Your witness.

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:02.900
- Now, Mrs. Lewis, you stated
that Michael was worried

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:05.273
about Jennifer, yet in
the week following--

00:16:05.273 --> 00:16:07.280
- Objection, Your Honor.
- Sustained.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:08.680
- Yeah, that was an
argumentative question.

00:16:08.680 --> 00:16:10.240
- In your testimony, Mrs. Lewis,

00:16:10.240 --> 00:16:12.310
you claimed that Jennifer
wanted sole custody.

00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:13.143
But isn't it true--

00:16:13.143 --> 00:16:14.090
- Objection.
- Sustained.

00:16:14.090 --> 00:16:16.220
- Yeah, argumentative again.
- Keep trying, Mrs. Florrick.

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:18.030
You'll hit on it.
(Devin laughs)

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:21.010
- It's cheeky of the judge,
but they're right to object

00:16:21.010 --> 00:16:23.453
and right to sustain the objections.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:28.890
- Mrs. Lewis, who's idea was it

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:31.900
to have dinner, Jennifer's or Michael's?

00:16:31.900 --> 00:16:32.910
- Michael's.

00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:35.850
- So wouldn't that suggest
that his attitude had changed

00:16:35.850 --> 00:16:37.080
towards Jennifer.
- Objection.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:38.063
- Sustained.

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:41.850
- That one calls for speculation

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:43.910
about what someone else was thinking

00:16:44.780 --> 00:16:46.710
and also phrased in sort
of an argumentative way.

00:16:46.710 --> 00:16:49.780
She's combining two
different things together

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:52.250
to try and create an argument
through the questioning.

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:53.440
She can ask leading questions,

00:16:53.440 --> 00:16:56.260
because this is a cross-examination.

00:16:56.260 --> 00:16:58.960
So she can say, "Isn't it
true that," but the way

00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:02.620
that she's combining things
together is an argumentative way

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:03.853
to phrase a question.

00:17:04.730 --> 00:17:07.770
It's a little ticky-tacky,
but they're right to object,

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:09.920
and the judge is right to
sustain the objections.

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.160
- So when an officer's
referring to dropping something

00:17:12.160 --> 00:17:15.160
in the pit or pitting
it, he refers to what?

00:17:15.160 --> 00:17:17.340
- Excluding it from the
crime scene narrative,

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:20.160
but that only applies
to irrelevant details.

00:17:20.160 --> 00:17:22.230
We wouldn't exclude pertinent evidence.

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:23.540
- Was anything pitted

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:26.080
from the Lewis crimes?
- Your Honor, objection!

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:27.610
Evidence is logged in all the time

00:17:27.610 --> 00:17:29.000
that proves irrelevant to the discovery.

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:30.340
It's not an intent to deceive.

00:17:30.340 --> 00:17:31.220
It's an intent

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:32.053
to decipher facts.
- We don't even know

00:17:32.053 --> 00:17:33.470
if it is anything.
- And to blame the prosecution

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:34.303
for not coming up--
- She's trying to build a case

00:17:34.303 --> 00:17:35.136
on a staple.
- With every single

00:17:35.136 --> 00:17:36.037
possible detail.
- That staple

00:17:36.037 --> 00:17:36.870
can mean anything.
- Okay, okay, Mrs. Florrick,

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:39.640
none of this was in the first trial.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:42.010
Is it your intention to
pursue a new defense?

00:17:42.010 --> 00:17:43.370
- Yes, Your Honor.

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:45.330
- So it's possible, if a judge knows

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:46.990
that there's gonna be
some contentious issues,

00:17:46.990 --> 00:17:49.470
that they bring the
counselors into their chambers

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:50.980
to discuss them in person.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:54.470
However, it is highly unusual
for either the prosecutors

00:17:54.470 --> 00:17:57.240
or the defense attorneys
to yell at the judge,

00:17:57.240 --> 00:17:59.120
especially two lawyers yelling

00:17:59.120 --> 00:18:00.450
at the same time at the judge.

00:18:00.450 --> 00:18:01.500
You really don't want

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:04.540
to piss off the judge in a criminal case

00:18:04.540 --> 00:18:07.340
where the judge is
presiding over your case.

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:09.900
And you don't want to get on
the bad side of the judge.

00:18:09.900 --> 00:18:11.220
- We all know what's going on here.

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:14.390
Peter Florrick was a corrupt
and convicted state's attorney.

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:16.470
If evidence was buried, he buried it.

00:18:16.470 --> 00:18:18.530
And now, she's benefiting
from his knowledge.

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:21.690
- Which still leaves you
with some pages missing here.

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:23.380
I'll give you till Monday.

00:18:23.380 --> 00:18:26.960
- Yeah, if the prosecutors
have hidden evidence

00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:30.420
from the defense, that
is totally improper.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:33.920
If there's a missing
part of a police report

00:18:33.920 --> 00:18:37.110
or a coroner's report, that's
not ancillary evidence.

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:39.240
That's not a tiny, small thing

00:18:39.240 --> 00:18:41.320
that may or may not be
relevant to the case.

00:18:41.320 --> 00:18:43.310
And also, the prosecutors are not the ones

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:44.800
who are allowed to make the call

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:47.370
as to what is relevant
and what isn't relevant.

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:48.740
That's up to the judge.

00:18:48.740 --> 00:18:51.240
So they should hot have been
withholding this paperwork.

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:56.240
That is Discovery 101,
where that information has

00:18:56.460 --> 00:18:57.640
to be turned over to the other side.

00:18:57.640 --> 00:19:00.070
You can't hide the ball
against the defense like that.

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:01.480
That's crazy.

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:02.930
- I interviewed the first jury,

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:04.850
and they voted 11 to one to convict.

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:05.850
- Excuse me, that's not true.

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:06.780
It was evenly split.

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:08.820
- No, half the jurors switched their votes

00:19:08.820 --> 00:19:11.410
when they couldn't get a
troubled juror to deliberate.

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:15.053
So I used my judgment
to change strategies.

00:19:16.840 --> 00:19:19.063
- And was it your
judgment not to update us?

00:19:19.063 --> 00:19:21.646
(solemn music)

00:19:24.610 --> 00:19:29.090
- So usually, in a big firm,
you would have a senior counsel

00:19:29.090 --> 00:19:31.130
or senior partner
looking over the shoulder

00:19:31.130 --> 00:19:33.700
of a junior associate
handling a high-profile,

00:19:33.700 --> 00:19:36.150
high-stakes case like
this criminal case here.

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:39.860
Here, the senior partners
don't seem to care,

00:19:39.860 --> 00:19:42.490
except that she didn't
check in, down the line.

00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:44.420
I mean, you can have it
one way or the other.

00:19:44.420 --> 00:19:46.900
You can either give your
associated free reign,

00:19:46.900 --> 00:19:48.340
or you can help her

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:51.860
and make sure she doesn't make
horrible malpractice mistakes

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:54.000
as she conducts this criminal defense.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:56.235
But, man, you got to choose one, I guess.

00:19:56.235 --> 00:19:58.185
- Could I have the monitors in, please?

00:20:02.470 --> 00:20:03.470
Thank you.

00:20:03.470 --> 00:20:06.780
- State's attorney here.
- Now, Mr. North.

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:08.100
- They're worried she's getting this stuff

00:20:08.100 --> 00:20:09.010
from her husband.

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:10.220
- There are three images.

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:12.500
The middle is the image
of the surveillance

00:20:12.500 --> 00:20:15.030
from the 15th, the night of the murder.

00:20:15.030 --> 00:20:17.060
And the one over there,
on the left, is the image

00:20:17.060 --> 00:20:19.030
from the 14th, the
night before the murder.

00:20:19.030 --> 00:20:21.220
And the one on the right is from the 16th,

00:20:21.220 --> 00:20:22.460
the night after the murder.

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:24.208
Can you see the dates on those?

00:20:24.208 --> 00:20:26.000
- Uh, yes, I can.

00:20:26.000 --> 00:20:28.500
- Generally, she wouldn't be
able to just tell the witness

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:31.180
that that evidence is what it is.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:33.050
She would have to lay the foundation

00:20:33.050 --> 00:20:36.420
that she has some basis to
show that that is the case.

00:20:36.420 --> 00:20:38.880
She could ask him to read the date stamps,

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:41.570
or I guess, get it
admitted through the judge

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:45.330
and get it stipulated that
that evidence is what it says.

00:20:45.330 --> 00:20:47.580
But you can't just say this is this

00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:48.900
and this is another thing

00:20:48.900 --> 00:20:52.710
and get the witness to
take your word for it.

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:54.010
- Either you have a plastic bag

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:57.520
that blows across your
lot every night at 11:48,

00:20:57.520 --> 00:20:59.351
or these are duplicates of the same tape.

00:20:59.351 --> 00:21:01.851
(tense music)

00:21:11.820 --> 00:21:14.060
- That's technically an
argumentative question.

00:21:14.060 --> 00:21:15.454
I mean, it's actually not--
- No, it's not

00:21:15.454 --> 00:21:17.210
what it looks like.
- Really a question at all.

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:18.580
- I understand, sir.

00:21:18.580 --> 00:21:20.780
You didn't willfully mislead the police.

00:21:20.780 --> 00:21:21.800
- Yeah, that's correct.

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:23.920
- No, it's just that
it gets cold out there,

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:27.520
and sometimes you don't
make the circuit of the lot.

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:28.550
- Yes.

00:21:28.550 --> 00:21:30.910
- So on the nights that you don't go out,

00:21:30.910 --> 00:21:33.220
you don't record the
actual surveillance image.

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:36.070
You set your computer up to
duplicate the night before,

00:21:36.070 --> 00:21:37.810
just in case your manager checks it.

00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:38.643
Is that correct?

00:21:40.360 --> 00:21:41.572
- Yes.
- Oh snap!

00:21:41.572 --> 00:21:43.510
You got lawyered, son!
- Just so I'm clear,

00:21:43.510 --> 00:21:46.160
there is not recording,
the night of the murder,

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:47.160
and you were never there

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:50.694
to see or not see the pickup
truck or the carjacker?

00:21:50.694 --> 00:21:53.010
(tense music)

00:21:53.010 --> 00:21:55.673
- I'm sorry, uh, yes.

00:21:56.810 --> 00:21:59.230
- So a classic Perry Mason moment

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:01.760
where the witness recants on the stand.

00:22:01.760 --> 00:22:03.850
Here, it's not a criminal defendant,

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:06.900
but it is the star witness, and he shows

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:10.410
that the really important
evidence has been doctored,

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:12.450
and it leads to the
defense showing their case.

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:16.760
I mean, that's not very realistic,
just because it's so rare

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:19.890
that people actually do
these kinds of actions

00:22:19.890 --> 00:22:22.300
that result in improper conviction.

00:22:22.300 --> 00:22:24.010
But the kind of investigation

00:22:24.010 --> 00:22:25.870
that Alicia has done here is good.

00:22:25.870 --> 00:22:28.670
She went to the particular crime scene.

00:22:28.670 --> 00:22:32.060
She interviewed all the
important witnesses.

00:22:32.060 --> 00:22:33.500
That's what a good lawyer does.

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:34.720
It's like an investigation.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:36.950
So I think the outcome is unrealistic,

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:39.090
but the underlying investigation is good.

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:41.621
That's what a good attorney should do.

00:22:41.621 --> 00:22:44.520
(laughs) And it worked out in this case,

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:47.723
but not gonna find too many of
those examples in real life.

00:22:48.719 --> 00:22:51.290
- So you're wondering

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:53.860
whether demolishing the
key prosecution witness

00:22:53.860 --> 00:22:56.280
didn't just save your ass as first chair.

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:57.700
Nice work, by the way.

00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:58.630
- Thanks.

00:22:58.630 --> 00:23:00.780
- But was it enough for reasonable doubt?

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:02.090
- Yes!

00:23:02.090 --> 00:23:05.990
She didn't just demolish the
prosecution's main witness.

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:08.800
She showed him to be an outright liar,

00:23:08.800 --> 00:23:11.990
that the jury can't trust
anything that man says,

00:23:11.990 --> 00:23:14.600
and she has removed a major impediment

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:17.160
to her case, which is that the story

00:23:17.160 --> 00:23:19.460
about the red truck
couldn't be corroborated.

00:23:19.460 --> 00:23:20.990
Well, the reason it
couldn't be corroborated is

00:23:20.990 --> 00:23:24.770
because the prosecution's
main witness was a liar.

00:23:24.770 --> 00:23:26.800
So yeah, if you're the prosecution,

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:27.840
you should be really worried,

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:30.180
because if your star
witness has just been shown

00:23:30.180 --> 00:23:33.510
to be a liar and nothing
he says can be trusted,

00:23:33.510 --> 00:23:35.820
you should probably pick
up your ball and go home,

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:39.350
because that should be
reasonable doubt right there.

00:23:39.350 --> 00:23:41.770
Reasonable doubt is a very high burden.

00:23:41.770 --> 00:23:45.000
And if your star witness has
just been shown to be a liar,

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:47.060
and the prosecution might have been shown

00:23:47.060 --> 00:23:48.890
to be covering up evidence,

00:23:48.890 --> 00:23:51.550
well, that should be
reasonable doubt right there.

00:23:51.550 --> 00:23:53.650
So (chuckles), I think she did a good job,

00:23:53.650 --> 00:23:55.310
and she has created reasonable doubt.

00:23:55.310 --> 00:23:57.206
She shouldn't be too worried
about that, right now.

00:23:57.206 --> 00:23:59.450
(solemn music)

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:00.990
- Mrs. Lewis, did you

00:24:00.990 --> 00:24:04.030
and the deceased sign
a prenuptial agreement?

00:24:04.030 --> 00:24:06.410
- Uh, yes, for tax purposes.

00:24:06.410 --> 00:24:09.320
- So if the deceased were
to divorce you, let say,

00:24:09.320 --> 00:24:11.020
in order to reunite with his first wife--

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:12.320
- Objection.
- Sustained.

00:24:13.820 --> 00:24:16.070
- If the deceased were to divorce you,

00:24:16.070 --> 00:24:18.160
you would be cut off from
his premarital savings,

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:19.660
is that correct?
- Objection.

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:22.870
- So I think that's relevant.

00:24:22.870 --> 00:24:25.840
She may not know that calls
for a legal conclusion,

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.220
which a lay witness can't do.

00:24:28.220 --> 00:24:30.000
But I think that the
prosecutor's objecting

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.980
on the basis of relevance, which, I mean,

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:35.900
she's laying the foundation for motive,

00:24:35.900 --> 00:24:37.700
which makes it relevant,

00:24:37.700 --> 00:24:40.270
or a counter-motive of this other witness.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:43.240
So I don't think that's
the right objection here.

00:24:43.240 --> 00:24:46.150
Also, if that is Alicia's
goal here, to show

00:24:46.150 --> 00:24:49.770
that another witness has a
motive to commit this crime,

00:24:49.770 --> 00:24:51.100
she might as well just come out

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:52.800
and argue against the objection,

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.367
and say, "Your Honor, it is relevant,

00:24:54.367 --> 00:24:56.477
"because it goes to the
motive of this witness

00:24:56.477 --> 00:24:59.230
"who is a possible other
suspect in this case."

00:24:59.230 --> 00:25:00.290
And just come right out,

00:25:00.290 --> 00:25:03.610
and so she could plant those
seeds in the jury's mind,

00:25:03.610 --> 00:25:06.120
that all of these questions
that she's going to ask go

00:25:06.120 --> 00:25:08.120
to whether this witness could also

00:25:08.120 --> 00:25:09.570
have orchestrated this crime.

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:11.730
So she might as well
just come out and say it,

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:14.560
because the more time she
spends attacking this witness

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:15.860
without the jury thinking

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:18.920
that she's another suspect
makes her look like a monster.

00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:20.870
And you don't want to lose
that rapport with the jury.

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:22.940
So she might as well
just make the argument

00:25:22.940 --> 00:25:25.630
and say she's another
suspect in this crime.

00:25:25.630 --> 00:25:29.490
- The previously unreleased
trace evidence is also admitted

00:25:29.490 --> 00:25:31.830
into evidence, the crime labs finding

00:25:31.830 --> 00:25:33.810
that these greyhound hairs recovered

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:36.840
in a chemical compound
called Alco Ectolin.

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:37.820
Have you heard of this?

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:38.870
- Sorry, the chemical?

00:25:38.870 --> 00:25:40.500
No.
- No, neither had I.

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:41.860
It is a lotion.

00:25:41.860 --> 00:25:45.017
It is a lotion that is
used at dog racing tracks

00:25:45.017 --> 00:25:46.360
to ease--
- Objection, not in evidence.

00:25:46.360 --> 00:25:49.830
- Sustained, and you might want
to stay standing, Mr. Brody.

00:25:49.830 --> 00:25:52.333
I have a feeling we're
nearing your smokescreen.

00:25:53.670 --> 00:25:56.810
- Mrs. Lewis, isn't it
a fact that a year ago,

00:25:56.810 --> 00:25:59.340
at the time of the murder,
your brother worked

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:00.960
at a dog track?
- Objection!

00:26:00.960 --> 00:26:02.400
- Sustained.

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:03.307
- No further questions.

00:26:03.307 --> 00:26:05.460
(piano music)
- So number one,

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:07.750
the prosecutor's objection
shouldn't be sustained.

00:26:07.750 --> 00:26:09.250
That evidence should come in.

00:26:09.250 --> 00:26:12.290
Number two, Alicia should
come out and fight like hell

00:26:12.290 --> 00:26:13.590
so that evidence can come in,

00:26:13.590 --> 00:26:15.250
because if she doesn't
get that evidence in

00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:18.180
through another witness,
the jury is gonna be told

00:26:18.180 --> 00:26:21.950
to ignore everything that
was struck from the record,

00:26:21.950 --> 00:26:24.250
which in this case was most of what

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:25.580
that woman just testified to.

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:28.610
Also, I'm not sure why
she's testifying again,

00:26:28.610 --> 00:26:30.240
unless she was called
as a rebuttal witness.

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:32.180
Usually, you have to ask
all of your questions

00:26:32.180 --> 00:26:33.793
for a particular witness, at one time.

00:26:33.793 --> 00:26:37.040
You don't get to call her back days

00:26:37.040 --> 00:26:39.890
after the first time,
you get to call her back.

00:26:39.890 --> 00:26:41.948
So there's a lot of
wonkiness going on here.

00:26:41.948 --> 00:26:44.531
(phone buzzes)

00:26:46.150 --> 00:26:47.790
- Hello.

00:26:47.790 --> 00:26:48.623
Yes.

00:26:49.810 --> 00:26:51.030
Thanks.

00:26:51.030 --> 00:26:51.863
- Jury's in?

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:55.950
- Wait, so the (sighs), okay,

00:26:55.950 --> 00:26:57.020
so the jury came back.

00:26:57.020 --> 00:26:59.240
That's crazy, because if she knows

00:26:59.240 --> 00:27:02.550
that the brother of that
woman worked at the dog track

00:27:02.550 --> 00:27:05.340
and may have been the
reason why there was hair,

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:06.570
you would follow that down.

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:08.040
So all the good investigative work

00:27:08.040 --> 00:27:09.100
that she did at the beginning

00:27:09.100 --> 00:27:11.940
of this episode is
totally belied by the fact

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:15.050
that she didn't follow up
on exculpatory evidence.

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:17.250
It sounds like, I'm guessing, by the way,

00:27:17.250 --> 00:27:18.370
that they showed the police officer,

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:19.830
that he's done their work for them.

00:27:19.830 --> 00:27:23.800
But holy crap, you have a possible reason

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:25.770
why someone else has
committed this murder,

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:27.300
and you're not following up on it.

00:27:27.300 --> 00:27:30.070
That is not good criminal defense work.

00:27:30.070 --> 00:27:33.030
- Chicago Homicide has decided
to reopen its investigation

00:27:33.030 --> 00:27:35.010
into the murder of Michael Lewis.

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:37.700
Detective Briggs, doing
an admirable amount

00:27:37.700 --> 00:27:39.360
of due diligence, has confirmed

00:27:39.360 --> 00:27:41.560
that Cindy Lewis' brother Danny had access

00:27:41.560 --> 00:27:44.440
to his employer's pickup
truck, the week of the murder.

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:48.120
And the dog hairs admitted
into evidence match those found

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:49.700
at his workplace.

00:27:49.700 --> 00:27:51.700
So our state's attorney--
- Damn!

00:27:51.700 --> 00:27:53.850
- In his radiant wisdom,

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:57.000
has decided to withdraw the
charges against your client

00:27:57.000 --> 00:28:00.000
and pursue a case against
Mrs. Lewis' brother.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:01.920
Isn't that right, Mr. Brody?

00:28:01.920 --> 00:28:04.490
All I need is a yes or no.

00:28:04.490 --> 00:28:05.590
- Yes.

00:28:05.590 --> 00:28:07.110
- Good.

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:11.130
- So the judge's belligerence
aside, prosecutors

00:28:11.130 --> 00:28:14.830
and US attorneys are not out
there just to get convictions.

00:28:14.830 --> 00:28:16.990
They want the truth as
much as anybody else.

00:28:16.990 --> 00:28:19.810
And in real life, if, I think (laughs),

00:28:19.810 --> 00:28:22.620
maybe not in all cases, but
probably in a case like this,

00:28:22.620 --> 00:28:26.440
if the prosecutor learned
of true exculpatory evidence

00:28:26.440 --> 00:28:29.780
and strong evidence that
someone else was responsible

00:28:29.780 --> 00:28:32.300
for a crime as heinous as murder,

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:35.240
they would have no problem
at all dropping the charges

00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:37.700
against this person and
pursuing those charges

00:28:37.700 --> 00:28:40.430
against the other suspect.

00:28:40.430 --> 00:28:42.250
Prosecutors have wide discretion

00:28:42.250 --> 00:28:45.180
as to what cases they bring,
when they end those cases,

00:28:45.180 --> 00:28:46.410
when they withdraw those cases

00:28:46.410 --> 00:28:48.750
and when they bring those
cases against somebody else.

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:50.610
So it makes absolute sense

00:28:50.610 --> 00:28:52.410
that the prosecutor would drop their case

00:28:52.410 --> 00:28:54.900
against this poor woman
and then pursue the case

00:28:54.900 --> 00:28:56.380
against this brother figure

00:28:56.380 --> 00:28:59.340
who it seems actually
committed this murder.

00:28:59.340 --> 00:29:02.360
And there wouldn't be
this hemming and hawing

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:04.410
by this prosecutor here.

00:29:04.410 --> 00:29:07.610
He would drop the case and
go against the other person.

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:08.840
- Then we're done here.

00:29:08.840 --> 00:29:10.820
(bright music)

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:12.350
- We're done here.

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:13.616
Good job, Alicia.

00:29:13.616 --> 00:29:15.463
(bright music)

00:29:15.463 --> 00:29:17.657
(Jennifer sighs)

00:29:17.657 --> 00:29:18.490
- Thank you.

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:19.810
Thank you so much.

00:29:19.810 --> 00:29:21.180
- All right, that was the good wife.

00:29:21.180 --> 00:29:22.220
That was pretty good.

00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:24.100
I enjoyed that a lot.

00:29:24.100 --> 00:29:28.180
They are better on objections
than most legal TV shows.

00:29:28.180 --> 00:29:30.340
The facts are totally unrealistic.

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:33.060
This happens over the
span of like three days,

00:29:33.060 --> 00:29:35.670
when in reality, it probably
would've taken months

00:29:35.670 --> 00:29:37.600
for this trial to proceed.

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:39.300
They were calling back witnesses

00:29:39.300 --> 00:29:41.670
when they had already been examined.

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:46.530
However, a lot of the legal
argument that was made was good.

00:29:46.530 --> 00:29:47.720
The investigation

00:29:47.720 --> 00:29:50.940
that the main character
undertook was good.

00:29:50.940 --> 00:29:53.780
She was saved by the
sort of deus ex machina.

00:29:53.780 --> 00:29:57.570
But still, nonetheless, a
better than average legal drama.

00:29:57.570 --> 00:30:00.821
So I would give The Good
Wife a solid B for accuracy.

00:30:00.821 --> 00:30:01.680
(dramatic music)

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:04.630
You're on the right track,
do better next time.

00:30:04.630 --> 00:30:06.900
So if you liked this
video, let me know about

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:09.130
what show I should do
next, in the comments,

00:30:09.130 --> 00:30:11.230
and check out this playlist
that I put together

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:13.990
which compiles a lot of
my other reaction videos.

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:16.230
So check out this
playlist, and I'll see you

00:30:16.230 --> 00:30:19.253
in the next one.
(gentle music)

